crossing the channel is the dangerous  part of coming to britain illegally but  once here many migrants tell us the  process of claiming asylum is nothing  like they imagined  we've gained an insight into a system  that appears overwhelmed well over a  hundred thousand asylum claims are  currently being dealt with including 32  year old journalist hushang armin and  iraqi kurd  after waiting in a camp in northern  france four months ago like thousands of  others he paid smugglers to bring him  across the channel by boat  huxiang speaks five languages including  english which makes him a popular figure  amongst the other migrants he lives with  in a hotel he tells us many find the  system too difficult to navigate by  themselves most of them they need help  and i will help them always because they  they can't speak english that's a big  problem of refugees for bank account for  nhs for gp for doctor  for home office sometimes they have a  big problem sometimes they run into my  room  doing like that when i open the door  they say please that's english  organization that is gp can you help do  you think some people get lost in the  system exactly  yes most of them to find out what it's  like to be stuck in the asylum system we  went to two different places in the  north of england to speak to migrants  and local residents we're not revealing  exactly where and this is why look as  you can see there's lots and lots of  migrants there  it's absolutely outrageous that we're  paying for this  absolute shocking this is videos like  this have been uploaded as recently as  august by the far-right group britain  first angry the uk government is  providing asylum seekers with food and  somewhere to live whilst their claims  are being processed  hi guys  what country is all from hi  are you staying in this hotel the  extremists have been targeting migrant  accommodation to make their voices heard  asylum seekers are not allowed to work  whilst their applications are being  processed but the number waiting more  than six months for a decision has gone  up tenfold in the last decade leaving  the applicants increasingly rudderless  and as we found that can sometimes put a  strain on relations with the local  community  peter lives near the hotel we visited  housing asylum seekers he says the  migrants should be sent back to their  own country although i was thinking for  my kid's safety i mean when you see them  walking around here and that's like a  park but i think my kids don't park and  i don't like to think i don't know they  are where they're from  and stuff you know what i mean but  there's no evidence that anyone's caused  any problems yeah i know what you're  saying yeah but it's just my opinion as  well where to house asylum seekers is a  minefield we went to a complex in the  leafy suburbs of another city  illustrating the lottery of the housing  dilemma  after they arrive in the uk asylum  seekers are dispersed around the country  they can end up in all sorts of  accommodation whether it's a repurposed  empty building shared housing or a hotel  i know people that have been in the  asylum process for several years and  have been moved round three or four uk  cities  i have  no family here we met bazaar from iran  separated from his friend who he crossed  the channel with nine months ago  i was i live in ronkon sometimes i come  here visit from my friend because i'm  alone in the city you know but however  wearing the process it doesn't seem to  be deterring the migrants  very difficult life from before  i this now is better thank you for god  thank you for england for government  england for the people helping for the  people  we were given this footage by asylum  seekers living in a former army barracks  in the south of england  they're sleeping areas separated by  curtains  those who fled conflict say being sent  to an ex-military site is difficult to  deal with psychologically it's another  glimpse into a system which campaigners  say desperately needs to be overhauled  the way to stop the delays is to process  the cranes of the people who are  entitled to asylum and get them working  they didn't ask to be put in hotels for  months what they want is to work and get  on with their lives the faster we get  them doing that the more we don't have  the delays and have people in the  hotels migrants are elated when they  finally set foot on british soil but  still ahead navigating a system which  even the government admits is broken  the home office says it takes the  well-being of asylum seekers and the  communities in which they live extremely  seriously and is determined to clear the  backlog of applications  some face lengthy appeals and whilst the  risk of deportation is low the  unsuccessful may end up living in the uk  illegally never actually part of a  society they risk their lives to try to  join lisa holland sky news
 

